WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.496
The Apple Silicon path: MLX-LM.

2
00:00:02.776 --> 00:00:09.709
On a Mac with Apple Silicon, MLX-LM does the same job natively, using the machine’s unified memory.

3
00:00:09.989 --> 00:00:13.979
Install it with its training extra, put your data in a folder as train.

4
00:00:14.259 --> 00:00:15.560
jsonl, valid.

5
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.869
jsonl (optional, for validation loss), and test.

6
00:00:19.149 --> 00:00:22.136
jsonl (for --test ), and run mlx_lm.

7
00:00:22.416 --> 00:00:23.355
lora --model.

8
00:00:23.635 --> 00:00:24.637
--train --data.

9
00:00:24.917 --> 00:00:33.472
It accepts chat data (a messages list, where the last message is the completion), tool-calling data, prompt-completion pairs, and plain text.

10
00:00:33.752 --> 00:00:36.248
--mask-prompt restricts the loss to the completion.

11
00:00:36.528 --> 00:00:41.307
If the model you point at is quantized, the run is QLoRA automatically.

12
00:00:41.587 --> 00:00:42.717
mlx_lm.

13
00:00:42.997 --> 00:00:47.072
generate takes --adapter-path to test the adapter, and mlx_lm.

14
00:00:47.352 --> 00:00:49.272
fuse merges it into the model.

15
00:00:49.552 --> 00:00:53.435
Its defaults differ from PEFT’s in ways that matter when you compare runs.

16
00:00:53.715 --> 00:00:54.888
Reading the 0.

17
00:00:55.168 --> 00:00:56.107
32.

18
00:00:56.387 --> 00:01:00.376
2 source on the review date: rank 8, a direct scale of 20.

19
00:01:00.656 --> 00:01:11.643
0 (not alpha over r), adapters on the linear layers of the last 16 blocks, learning rate 1e-5, batch size 4, and 1,000 iterations.

20
00:01:11.923 --> 00:01:16.872
LoRA settings such as rank and scale go in a YAML config file passed with -c.

21
00:01:17.152 --> 00:01:26.880
The docs’ memory tips: use a quantized model, lower the batch size with gradient accumulation, adapt fewer layers, and turn on gradient checkpointing.

22
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:28.867
The lab’s mlx_finetune.

23
00:01:29.147 --> 00:01:32.624
py mirrors the notebook’s settings (rank 16, scale 2.

24
00:01:32.904 --> 00:01:43.336
0, all 24 blocks, learning rate 2e-4) so the two runs are comparable, and writes every train and validation loss to a JSONL file your parser reads.

25
00:01:43.616 --> 00:01:50.165
During authoring, a 150-iteration run of it on an Apple Silicon Mac took about a minute and a half.
